<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 5px;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping in the Garden</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Tomatoes Grow Quickly.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Express opinions about familiar topics in writing.<br>interactive: Write simple role plays or dialogues collaboratively.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can express opinions in writing about garden tasks in short sentences (e.g., 'I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging.').<br>Can write simple role plays or dialogues about garden tasks with classmates (e.g., 'How do you plant the tomatoes?' 'You dig a hole, plant the seed, and water the plant.').<br>Can collaborate to revise dialogues for clarity (e.g., Add 'water the plants' to the dialogue.).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> Garden Word Scramble Race.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher prepares cards with scrambled garden words (e.g., 'tmoato', 'edse', 'ogrw', 'liadg', 'iol', 'tnepl').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs or small groups. They receive a set of scrambled words and unscramble them, writing the correct words on their whiteboards or paper.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The first group to correctly unscramble and write all words wins. This activates vocabulary and focuses on spelling/writing.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows one scrambled word (e.g., 'tneraigw anc'). "Look, this word is mixed up. What is it? A watering can! I will write 'watering can' here." The teacher explicitly writes the correct word on the board, enunciating '/wɑːtərɪŋ kæn/'. The teacher also models how to spell out the words ‘water’ and ‘soil’ to reinforce phonemic awareness for /t/ and /g/ sounds. "W-A-T-E-R. S-O-I-L."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are we drawing pictures or writing words?" (Writing words)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Are the words about animals or the garden?" (The garden)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short written dialogue (A1.2 level) between two children, Leo and Maya, talking about planting tomatoes in their garden. The dialogue includes opinions about tasks, simple instructions, and uses present simple + 's', demonstratives, sequence words, and 'and', 'but'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>Example Dialogue:</i><br><b>Leo:</b> We need to plant these tomatoes. This is a big plant!<br><b>Maya:</b> Yes! I like planting seeds, but I don't like digging big holes.<br><b>Leo:</b> I like digging! First, I dig a hole. Then, I put the plant in. Do you like watering?<br><b>Maya:</b> Yes, I always water the plants every day. It's fun! My mom says the tomatoes grow quickly when I water them.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describing simple gardening instructions and expressing preferences in writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students read the dialogue and answer simple questions in writing.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who likes digging? (Leo likes digging.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is the first thing Leo does? (First, he digs a hole.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Does Maya like digging? (No, she doesn't like digging.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How often does Maya water the plants? (She waters the plants every day.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice writing sentences expressing preferences (like/don't like), describing routines (Present simple + 's', adverbs of frequency), using demonstratives, question forms, and sequence words. Focus on correct spelling and sentence structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice).</b> The teacher provides worksheets with incomplete sentences related to gardening. Students fill in the blanks using target vocabulary (grow, water, plant, dig, seed, soil, tomato) and grammatical structures (like/don't like, Present simple + 's', every day, sometimes, always).<br><i>Examples:</i><br>a. I _________ watering the plants. (like / don't like)<br>b. My brother _________ (dig) holes for the seeds.<br>c. We _________ (plant) new _________ in the _________.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Combining Sentences (Semi-controlled).</b> Students are given two short, simple sentences and rewrite them as one using 'and' or 'but' to express routines or preferences, and sequence words 'first', 'second'. This reinforces discourse markers and sociolinguistic competence.<br><i>Examples:</i><br>a. I water the plants. They grow quickly. -> I water the plants, and they grow quickly.<br>b. He likes to dig. She doesn't like to dig. -> He likes to dig, but she doesn't like to dig.<br>c. You dig a hole. You plant the seed. -> First, you dig a hole, and second, you plant the seed.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Question and Answer Writing (Pair Work).</b> In pairs, students write 3-4 simple questions about garden tasks using 'Do you...?' and 'How often...?' or 'What are these/this?', and then write answers to their partner's questions. They exchange notebooks with another pair and check for clarity and correct grammar/vocabulary. This activity focuses on writing accurate question forms and answers, incorporating demonstratives.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping in the Garden".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Collaborative Dialogue Writing.</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in pairs or small groups (3 students).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to write a short dialogue (minimum 6 lines) between two or three friends who are helping in the garden.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The dialogue must include:</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Expressing preferences (e.g., "I like watering the plants, but I don't like digging.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- Describing at least two gardening steps using sequence words (first, second) and conjunctions (and).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- At least one question about a garden task (e.g., "How often do you water the plants?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">- At least two garden vocabulary words (e.g., 'tomato', 'seed', 'soil').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to collaboratively write and revise their dialogues for clarity and completeness.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example prompt for students:</b> "Imagine you are in the garden with your friend. Write a dialogue about what you like and don't like to do, and how you plant a new flower or vegetable."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment using a simple checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their collaboratively written dialogues with another group. Each group uses a checklist provided by the teacher to assess the other group's dialogue.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Checklist items):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they express opinions (like/don't like)? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they describe gardening steps (e.g., 'First, ...')? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did they use at least two new garden words? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the dialogue easy to read and understand? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is there at least one question and answer? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was one sentence I wrote well today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What is one word I learned to spell better today?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What part of writing the dialogue was easy for my group?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What part of writing the dialogue was difficult for my group, and why?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this writing lesson? (e.g., Students actively collaborated to write dialogues; they successfully incorporated opinions and sequence words.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for students to improve their writing in the next lesson? (e.g., Some students struggled with subject-verb agreement for 's'; more guided practice on question formation in writing.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the lesson connect to the next lesson (Mediation & Integration)? (The next lesson will involve students summarizing or presenting their garden dialogues or instructions to another class or a wider audience, demonstrating their written and communicative skills in a new context.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>